        type: "p",
        text: "Composition makes the difference between snapshot and photograph. Rule of thirds: Divide frame into 3x3 grid. Place subject at intersection points (not center). Creates dynamic, balanced photos. Most cameras/phones have grid overlay. Leading lines: Use roads, fences, rivers, paths to lead eye to subject. Creates depth and guides viewer. Symmetry and patterns: Reflections in water, repeated architecture, geometric patterns. Satisfying, formal compositions. Framing: Use natural frames (doorways, windows, arches, trees) to frame subject. Adds depth, focuses attention. Foreground interest: Include interesting foreground (rocks, flowers, people) for landscape photos. Creates 3D feel, depth. Perspective: Don't always shoot at eye level. Get low (worm's eye), high (bird's eye), close up. Unique perspectives make ordinary scenes extraordinary. Negative space: Use empty space (sky, water, wall) around subject. Creates mood, emphasizes subject. Fill the frame: Get close. Don't leave wasted space around subject. Background: Check what's behind subject. Avoid distractions (poles growing from heads, clutter). Golden hour: Shoot first/last hour of sunlight. Warm, soft, directional light. Blue hour: 30 min before sunrise/after sunset. Magical blue tones. Avoid midday sun: Harsh shadows, washed out colors. Overcast days: Soft, even light. Great for portraits, flowers. Break rules: Once you know rules, break them intentionally for creative effect.",
